arc=add('lem-equicharacteristic-fixed-coordinate-blowup-chain-defines-formal-arc','A fixed-coordinate point-blowup chain defines a formal arc',['thm-completion-of-a-noetherian-local-ring','cor-equicharacteristic-complete-local-power-series-quotient','thm-one-dimensional-regular-local-rings-are-dvrs','thm-blowup-universal-property','thm-affine-blowup-standard-charts'],r'''Let $(B,\mathfrak m,\kappa)$ be an equicharacteristic Noetherian local domain and $(B_n,\mathfrak m_n)$ the local rings at a successive infinite chain of point blowups, all with residue field $\kappa$. Suppose one $t\in\mathfrak m$ generates the pullback of every centre ideal on the next local ring. Then there is a nonsingular formal arc $B\to\kappa[[t]]$, extended from a surjection $\widehat B\to\kappa[[t]]$, whose point-blowup centres are the given ones.''',r'''The pullback equality m_n B_(n+1)=tB_(n+1) implies t^j∉m_n^(j+1): such membership would give t^j∈t^(j+1)B_(n+1), making t a unit in this local domain. Choose generators y of m_n and write y=t a in B_(n+1). Lift the residue of a to c∈B_n and put z=y−ct; then z∈m_(n+1)² and m_n is generated by t and these z's. Put J_n=ker(B→B_n/m_n^(n+1)). They form a decreasing family. If f∈J_n, expand its image as a degree-(n+1) expression in t,z. Every term involving z belongs to m_(n+1)^(n+2), since z maps into m_(n+1)²; modulo that ideal f is c t^(n+1), with c lifted from B by equality of residue fields. The power t^(n+1) survives, so J_n/J_(n+1) has length one. Induction gives length(B/J_n)=n+1 and its maximal ideal generated by t. Also m^(n+1)⊂J_n, so these are finite quotients of the completed ring. Choose a coefficient field κ⊂Bhat using the equicharacteristic power-series-quotient supplier. In each quotient, its coefficient field and t identify B/J_n with κ[t]/(t^(n+1)), by Nakayama and the computed length. The identifications are compatible; therefore lim B/J_n=κ[[t]], and the map from Bhat is onto because it contains the coefficient field and t and is complete. To recover the local blowup lifts, use the t-chart at each step: its coordinates are y/t, and denominators not in the prescribed maximal ideal. For each later m, the coordinate numerator minus its prescribed residue times its denominator maps into m_m to one higher order than that denominator. The J_m calculation thus shows its image in κ[[t]] has nonnegative valuation and precisely that prescribed residue. The denominators outside the prescribed maximal ideal map to units, so the coordinate evaluations give local B_n→κ[[t]] compatible with B. The centre ideal maps to (t); the blowup universal property yields the same lifts, and equality of their residues gives exactly the prescribed centres. This argument uses equicharacteristic coefficient fields explicitly, and does not infer an inverse-limit surjection from length counts alone.''',source={'title':'The Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, Lemma 54.10.1, with coefficient-field and inverse-limit details for the equicharacteristic route','url':'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/resolve.pdf'})
termination=add('lem-normal-complete-surface-nonsingular-formal-arc-blowups-terminate','A nonsingular formal arc on a normal complete surface becomes regular',['thm-height-one-localisation-of-normal-noetherian-domain-is-dvr','lem-normal-domain-implies-s-two','lem-cm-local-codimension-and-regular-quotient-ext-concentration','thm-affine-blowup-standard-charts','thm-nakayama-lemma'],r'''Let $A$ be a complete equicharacteristic normal local domain of dimension two with a surjection onto a complete DVR $V$ inducing its residue-field identification. The successive point blowups along this nonsingular arc become regular at the arc centre after finitely many steps.''',r'''Let P=ker(A→V), choose t mapping to a V-uniformizer, and minimally generate P by u2,...,ur. Then m=(t,u2,...,ur). The CM dimension formula makes P height one, so A_P is a DVR. Since a generator of its maximal ideal must occur among the ui, relabel so u2 is a uniformizer there. The finite module P/(P²+(u2)) vanishes at P; as a finite module over A/P=V it is torsion, hence killed by a power of t. Thus for i>2 choose ni≥0 and ai∈A with t^ni ui−ai u2∈P². If ni=0, modulo mP this removes ui from the minimal generating family. If ai is a unit, it removes u2 instead. Otherwise ai mod P is either zero or a unit times t^mi with mi>0; absorb its P part into P². When all ni>0 and relevant mi>0, on the arc's t-chart set vi=ui/t. Dividing the relations by t² gives t^(ni−1)vi−unit·t^(mi−1)v2 in (v2,...,vr)², or t^(ni−1)vi in that square. Localize at the arc centre; the kernel of its surjection to V is generated by the vi and t remains a uniformizer. If the minimal number of generators drops, restart with that smaller number; its height-one localization is still A_P, since t is outside P and the blowups are isomorphisms there. Otherwise the displayed nonnegative exponents decrease at every step until one becomes zero, forcing a generator drop by Nakayama. There can only be finitely many drops. At the end P has one generator and m is generated by two elements; the local domain has dimension two by the modification dimension helper, hence is regular. This supplies the finite decreasing-invariant argument behind the source's short final paragraph.''',source={'title':'The Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, Lemma 54.10.2, full generator/exponent termination argument','url':'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/resolve.pdf'})
rows[termination]['deps']=sorted(set(rows[termination]['deps']+['lem-local-normal-surface-modification-dimension-and-projective-cohomology']))
square=add('lem-quadratic-in-a-square-ideal-with-nontrivial-colength-is-a-square','Quadratics in square ideals of colength greater than one',['def-derivation-algebra','def-kahler-differentials-algebra','thm-regular-local-rings-are-domains-and-cohen-macaulay','lem-surface-p-basis-subfield-separation'],r'''If $I\subset\kappa[x,y]$ has colength greater than one and contains a nonzero polynomial $q$ of total degree at most two in $I^2$, then $q$ is a scalar times the square of an affine linear polynomial. Infinite colength is allowed.''',r'''The two partial derivatives of q belong to I by Leibniz. A nonzero constant derivative would force I to be the unit ideal. If a nonconstant linear derivative is nonzero, make an affine coordinate change to put x∈I. Then I=(x) or (x,F(y)) with F monic of degree at least two. Modulo x², the x-coefficient of an element of I² is divisible by F and its constant-in-x part divisible by F². The degree bounds force both parts of q to vanish, leaving q=constant·x². If both derivatives vanish, characteristic is two and q=a+dx²+fy². A nonzero constant polynomial cannot lie in a proper I²; if only one variable occurs, differentiating its nonsquare scalar coefficient yields a unit in I unless the scalar ratio is a square, after which the result holds. Otherwise normalize f=1. If a and d are both squares, q is already a square. If not, a field derivation θ detecting a nonsquare exists by extending the dual derivations of a p-basis. Applying θ to q gives θ(a)+θ(d)x²∈I; θ(d)=0 would force a nonzero constant in I, so set α=θ(a)/θ(d). Then J=(x²+α,y²+a+dα)⊂I, and κ[x,y]/J has basis 1,x,y,xy. Since q is a nonzero linear combination of the two regular-sequence generators of J, it is not in J² (their conormal classes are a free basis, directly by division by their monic separate-variable polynomials). Thus I/J≠0. If it contains any nonzero vector with zero xy coefficient, I contains a nonzero affine linear polynomial and the earlier case applies. Otherwise I/J is one-dimensional and I has colength three. Extend scalars to an algebraic closure and translate x,y to make J=(x²,y²). Its one-dimensional added ideal is spanned by g+hx+iy+jxy with j≠0. If g≠0 it is a unit in that local four-dimensional algebra; if h or i is nonzero its ideal contains two independent vectors, so the quotient has length at most two. Both contradict length three. Thus Ibar=(x²,xy,y²). Its square has no nonzero polynomial of degree at most two, contradicting q∈Ibar². This excludes the remaining nonsquare case and proves the claim over the original field.''',source={'title':'The Stacks Project, Resolution of Surfaces, Lemma 54.12.1, with the characteristic-two colength calculation supplied','url':'https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/resolve.pdf'})
conic=add('lem-rational-gorenstein-surface-tangent-conic-and-hilbert-function','The tangent conic of a rational Gorenstein surface singularity',['lem-rational-normal-surface-point-blowup-normal-and-fibre-cohomology','lem-rational-singular-point-blowup-canonical-pullback-surjective','lem-regular-base-surface-cartier-curve-canonical-adjunction','thm-serre-duality-for-coherent-sheaves-on-projective-cm-scheme','cor-degree-additive-proper-curve','def-hilbert-function-and-hilbert-series','thm-cohomology-projective-space-twisting-sheaves'],r'''Let $A$ be a nonregular rational normal local surface domain in the permitted canonical-module setting, with $\omega_A\cong A$. Then its point blowup is normal with trivial canonical module. Its exceptional conormal $L$ has degree two and $\dim_\kappa\mathfrak m^n/\mathfrak m^{n+1}=2n+1$. For a minimal generating triple of $\mathfrak m$, the associated graded ring is $\kappa[T_1,T_2,T_3]/(q)$ for one nonzero quadratic $q$, so the exceptional fibre is its plane conic.''',r'''The canonical evaluation is a surjection from O_X to the torsion-free rank-one ω_X, so is an isomorphism; its kernel is generically zero and hence zero. The point-blowup helper gives H0(E,O)=κ, H1(E,O)=0 and H1(E,L^n)=0 for n≥0. Cartier adjunction gives ω_E=L^-1. Since L is globally generated and nontrivial, H0(L^-1)=0 by the product-of-sections argument, while Serre duality gives H1(L^-1)=H0(O)∨ of dimension one. Thus χ(L^-1)=-1 and χ(O)=1, giving deg L^-1=-2 and hence deg L=2 by inverse-degree additivity. Tensor-power additivity yields χ(L^n)=1+2n, and the fibre cohomology formula gives the stated Hilbert function. At n=1 it gives a minimal generating triple. The polynomial ring on their initial classes surjects onto gr_m A; at degree two its six-dimensional space maps onto a five-dimensional space, giving one nonzero q. Multiplication by that nonzero quadratic in the polynomial domain is injective, so the degree-n part of its quotient has dimension binom(n+2,2)−binom(n,2)=2n+1. The surjection from this quotient to gr_m A is therefore an isomorphism in every degree. Taking Proj identifies the exceptional fibre with the conic. This avoids applying a smooth-curve Riemann–Roch theorem to a nonreduced exceptional curve.''')
caseI=add('lem-nonsquare-tangent-conic-rational-surface-blowups-terminate','Nonsquare tangent-conic surface singularities terminate under point blowups',[conic,square,arc,termination,'lem-surface-regular-fibres-preserve-normality','lem-surface-completion-base-change-preserves-closed-fibre-local-completions','lem-rational-surface-local-rings-propagate-by-point-sequence-spreading'],r'''For a nonregular rational normal local surface domain in the permitted class with invertible canonical module and normal completion, if its tangent-conic quadratic is not a scalar times a square, repeatedly blowing up its singular points terminates in a regular model. Each blowup has at most one singular successor; that successor is residue-field rational and again has nonsquare tangent conic.''',r'''Choose generators x1,x2,x3 and lift the unique quadratic relation to Σ aij xi xj=Σ aijk xi xj xk. On the x1-chart put yi=xi/x1; dividing the relation gives the exceptional conic q(1,y2,y3). At a singular closed point p the fibre equation belongs to the square of its plane maximal ideal: otherwise quotienting by it lowers the two-dimensional plane cotangent space by one, so the surface local maximal ideal generated by x1 and two lifts would have embedding dimension at most two and be regular. If its residue degree exceeded one, the quadratic square-ideal lemma would force q to be a scalar square. Thus p is κ-rational. Change the triple to put p=(1,0,0); q then involves only T2,T3 and is a nonsquare binary quadratic. If it has a κ-root it splits into two distinct κ-lines, with unique singular point their intersection; otherwise the conic has only the rational vertex. Hence there is only one possible singular point. Absorb coefficients a11,a12,a13 with zero residues into the cubic part. Singularity of the chart further forces the constant cubic coefficient a111 to lie in m (else the chart relation eliminates x1 as a cotangent generator). Write it modulo (x2,x3) as b x1. The quadratic relation in the successor generators x1,y2,y3 then restricts on the plane x1=0 to precisely the same nonsquare binary quadratic in y2,y3; the other terms have an x1 factor. Its successor is therefore again case I, and its rational singular point cannot lie on x1=0. Thus the original fixed x1 cuts out the exceptional divisor at every point in a hypothetical infinite successor chain. The residue fields all remain κ. The fixed-coordinate helper constructs a nonsingular arc from the completion onto a complete DVR. Completion normality gives regularity at its height-one kernel; the formal-arc termination helper makes the completed local blowup chain regular after finitely many steps. Blowup flat base change and the equal closed-fibre local completions helper identify its completions with those of the original chain, so completion-preserved regularity gives the same regularity on the original chain, contradiction. There is only one branch, so the finite chain resolves all singular points in this case. No square-conic case is asserted.''')
rows[caseI]['deps']=sorted(set(rows[caseI]['deps']+['thm-completion-preserves-regular-local-rings','thm-blowup-base-change-flat']))
